  "tail": "```\n### FINDING 1 — FATAL — location: Remark 1 (\"Why regularity is defined for cardinals and not for ordinals\")\nCLAIM AS WRITTEN: \"it holds only at $0$, at $1$, and at those ordinals that are already infinite cardinals.\"\nWHY IT FAILS: The statement asserts that every infinite cardinal $\\kappa$ satisfies $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) = \\kappa$. This is false. The item under review itself defines an infinite cardinal to be **singular** when $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) \\ne \\kappa$, and later in the same page ([[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]]) it is shown that $\\aleph_\\omega$ is singular (i.e., $\\operatorname{cf}(\\aleph_\\omega) = \\aleph_0 \\ne \\aleph_\\omega$). In reality, an infinite cardinal satisfies $\\operatorname{cf}(\\kappa) = \\kappa$ if and only if it is regular; singular infinite cardinals do not satisfy it.\nWHAT WOULD FIX IT: Change to \"it holds only at $0$, at $1$, and at those ordinals that are regular infinite cardinals\" or \"the ordinals satisfying $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha)=\\alpha$ are exactly $0$, $1$, and the regular infinite cardinals.\"\n\n### FINDING 2 — NONFATAL — location: Remark 4 (\"Only one notion of cofinal exists in this library\")\nCLAIM AS WRITTEN: \"[[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]] introduces cofinal subsets, because the boundedness theorem for $\\omega_1$ needs them\"\nWHY IT FAILS: The claim asserts a dependency relationship with another item (the boundedness theorem for $\\omega_1$) whose text is not provided. It therefore cannot be verified. (The audit instructions explicitly note that remarks about how another item uses this one are frequent falsehoods and should not be passed over silently.)\nWHAT WOULD FIX IT: Either provide the boundedness theorem as a dependency so the claim can be checked, or remove the unverifiable historical remark.\n\n1 fatal, 1 nonfatal\n```"
